.TH EUCLID-CLI-ENS-UPDATE-MESSAGE-BODY 1 "November 2026" "euclid-cli 2.1.3" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ens-update-message-body \- replace the body of a published message
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ens update-message-body
.BR \-\-message\-id | \-m
.I MESSAGE-ID
.BR \-\-body | \-b
.I BODY
.SH DESCRIPTION
Replaces the body of a message already published to a topic. The whole
body is replaced, not part of it.
.PP
This changes the copy ENS holds: what
.BR euclid-cli-ens-list-messages (1)
and
.BR euclid-cli-ens-get-message (1)
answer with, and what
.BR euclid-cli-ens-resend-messages (1)
would send. The copies subscribers already received were delivered when
the message was published, and are not changed.
.PP
The topic's maximum message length applies as it does to
.BR euclid-cli-ens-publish-message (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-message\-id " " \fIMESSAGE-ID\fR
ID of the message to change. Required.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-body " " \fIBODY\fR
The new body. Required. A value starting with
.I file://
is read from the file it names instead of being taken literally.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The body was replaced. The message ID, the topic, the new size, the size
it replaced and the new content type are printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the server could not be reached, no message
with that ID exists, or the new body is longer than the topic allows.
.SH EXAMPLES
Correct a message, then deliver the corrected copy again:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ens update-message-body \e
    --message-id 6f1b1e2e-... --body file://./corrected.json
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ens (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-get-message (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-resend-messages (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-publish-message (1)
